<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?>
<SpikeArrest continueOnError="false" enabled="true" name="SA-SpikeArrest">
    <DisplayName>Per-caller burst protection</DisplayName>
    <!--
      A budget cap and a rate limit solve different problems and neither replaces
      the other.

      The budget says how much a user may spend in a day. It says nothing about
      how fast. A client stuck in a retry loop can burn a full daily budget in
      seconds - technically within policy, operationally a incident, and a
      guaranteed way to exhaust the shared Agent Platform project quota for everybody
      else in the process.

      Spike arrest is also among the cheapest policies in the chain, which is why
      it runs near the front: a caller in a hot loop is rejected before the
      gateway spends effort verifying signatures, reading configuration or calling
      countTokens.

      Identifier is the raw bearer token, extracted by EV-ExtractBearerToken but
      not yet verified. That ordering is deliberate: a flood of INVALID tokens is
      throttled too, instead of being allowed to hammer signature verification and
      the JWKS cache. The trade-off is that the throttle key is attacker-chosen -
      rotating the token string escapes it - so this is burst protection, not an
      access control. The budget counters, keyed on a verified claim, are the
      control.
    -->
    <Identifier ref="cap.auth.token"/>
    <Rate>@@SPIKE_ARREST_RATE@@</Rate>
    <UseEffectiveCount>true</UseEffectiveCount>
</SpikeArrest>
